ing to do what we did here in  reverse okay first thing I'm going to do  is I'm going to bring up all the  coefficients as exponents so if I see a  coefficient I'm just gonna bring it up  as a power here okay so I'm just  bringing those up okay  okay like that so in our first step what  we're gonna do is we're gonna write this  as natural log of 2 cubed which is 8  okay  - natural log of the square root of 5x  because the 1/2 power is the square root  okay  plus the natural log of the cube root of  7 because a 1/3 power is a cube root ok  minus the natural log of X okay plus the  natural log of 2x all to the fourth  power which is 16 X to the fourth minus  natural log of X minus 1 okay so we took  care of the coefficients we brought  those up as powers using the power rule  for logs but now what we want to do is  we want to look at everything that's  subtracted ok when you subtract logs  that's going to be the same as when you  divide them so it's the quotient rule  and you subtract you divide when you add  you multiply so the wood what happens  here is anything that's added is going  to be in the numerator multiplied  together those arguments are going to be  multiplied anything that's subtracted is  going to be divided it's gonna go in the  denominator and those arguments are  going to multiply together so we're  going to condense this all into one  natural log here so follow me on this  we've got natural log of 8 okay times  the cube root of 7 okay times 16x to the  fourth so those are all the positive  logs the ones that were added / ok the  ones we're subtracting which is the  square root of 5x okay  ax + X - 1 now I would continue to  simplify this down a little bit more 8  times 16 is 80 plus 48 is 128 so we have  the natural log of 128 X to the fourth  cube root of 7  all divided by the natural log is not  going to be divided okay it's just the  argument okay so that's a common mistake  students make X times X minus 1 times  the square root of 5x so this is 